The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().bind(this);
```
```java
// No unregistering of the download receiver
```
Reason:
The `DownloadManagerInterface` was bound to the main activity, which required the download receiver to be unregistered when the activity was destroyed. Since there was no code to unregister the receiver, it remained active, leading to potential memory leaks and unintended behavior after the activity was no longer in use.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```
Reason:
The `onDestroy` method is overridden to call `DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);`, which properly unregisters the download receiver when the activity is destroyed. This ensures that the receiver is no longer active, preventing memory leaks and unintended behavior associated with the activity's lifecycle.

